
========== INVARIANT-QUERY ROTATION PERFORMANCE TESTS ==========
Fixed world-space query: (0.0, 0.0, 1.0). The value/point dataset is fixed; only its orientation key varies.
A) Fixed Rotated KD-Tree: inverse-transform the invariant query and query the unchanged index.
B) Rotate points + array scan: physically rotate every point into a new temporary array, then search that array around the unchanged query.
C) Fixed uniform grid: retain points in fixed spatial hash cells; inverse-transform the invariant query and expand neighboring cells until the nearest result is proven.
Expected complexity for n points and m keys: A) O(n log n) build, O(m log n) expected / O(mn) worst-case lookup; B) O(mn) lookup and O(n) temporary array; C) O(n) grid build, typically near O(m) lookup for bounded cell occupancy / O(mn) worst case.
All three indexes/point sets require O(n) persistent space.
The spherical-coordinate triple (angle, axis azimuth, axis inclination) is the rotation key.


========== CHARACTER-TO-PARAGRAPH PERFORMANCE TESTS BEGIN ==========

Fixed dataset: 28 distinct values across 28 3D points; invariant query: (0.0, 0.0, 1.0); rotation sequence: 9,500 steps.
The KD-Tree indexes numeric 3D coordinates; an immutable parallel value table maps each point index to its character or whole word.
The stored orientation key is the spherical coordinate triple (rotation angle, axis azimuth, axis inclination), precomputed once per supported dataset value and reused for encoding.
AOT KEY ENCODING COMPARISON: calculate each value's orientation on demand versus reusing a precomputed value-to-key table. Both produce the same linked path.
A) ROTATED KD-TREE: keep the index and world query fixed; inverse-rotate the query for each lookup.
B) ROTATED ARRAY: allocate a rotated copy of all 28 value points for each step, then scan for the fixed query.
C) UNIFORM GRID / FIXED SPATIAL HASH: bucket the unchanged points into 0.25-unit 3D cells; inverse-rotate the query and search neighboring buckets outward until no unvisited cell can contain a closer point. This uses no KD-Tree.
All three methods use the same rotation sequence and must return the same value sequence.
AOT key preparation and message encoding (median):
  Build fixed dataset/tree:       0.000027 s
  D) Precompute 28 unique value keys once: 0.002914 s
  D) Encode 9,500 values, calculating each key: 0.362224 s
  D) Encode 9,500 values, reusing AOT keys: 0.002593 s
  D) AOT precompute + one encoding: 0.005506 s
  D) Encoding-only speedup after precompute: 139.70x
  D) Precompute amortizes after about 1 encoding(s) of this sequence length.
  D) Best for one encoding including setup: AOT precompute + encode.
  D) Best after table is built and reused: AOT precomputed keys.
MEASURED ELAPSED TIME (median of 5 runs)
  A) Build Rotated KD-Tree once:          0.000008 s
  A) Inverse-query + Rotated KD-Tree:     0.206285 s
  A) Build + query total:                 0.206294 s
  B) Rotate array + scan:                 0.119268 s
  C) Build uniform grid once:             0.000018 s
  C) Inverse-query + grid lookups:        0.322587 s
  C) Build + query total:                 0.322605 s
All three returned the exact same sequence.
Fastest lookup for this scenario: B) Rotate points + array scan.
Rotated KD-Tree complexity (fixed 3D space, n indexed values, m orientation queries):
  Build: O(n log n) expected time and O(n) index space for a balanced tree.
  One inverse rotation: O(1); one nearest-neighbor lookup: O(log n) expected in low dimensions, O(n) worst case.
  m lookups: O(m log n) expected, O(mn) worst case; including construction: O(n log n + m log n) expected.
  AOT key table: O(n) build time and O(n) space; encoding m values with precomputed keys: O(m) plus O(m) path storage.
Storage payload comparison (object/index overhead excluded):
Rotation-sequence storage payload (object overhead excluded)
  Encoded values' UTF-8 payload:  9,500 B
  Three float64 coordinates/step: 24 B
  Linked path rotation keys:      228,000 B
  AOT lookup table payload:       28 keys x 24 B = 672 B
  Spherical coordinates are orientation keys, not cryptographic hashes or compression.
Dataset/key storage break-even (payload only):
  Retain the base dataset plus orientation keys instead of one full rotated point snapshot per step when:
    N*P + M*H < M*N*P
  Equivalent for M>1: N > M*H / ((M-1)*P) = 1.000 points.
  This run: N=28, M=9500, P=24 B, H=24 B.
  Base + keys: 228,672 B; M snapshots: 6,384,000 B.
  Snapshot-storage break-even threshold: N > 1.000 points. This compares full rotated point snapshots, not compact strings of values.
  Concrete payload comparisons (base + keys vs. M full snapshots):
    N=1,000, M=100: 25.78 KiB vs. 2.29 MiB (90.9x less payload).
    N=100,000, M=1,000: 2.31 MiB vs. 2.24 GiB (990.1x less payload).
    N=1,000,000, M=100: 22.89 MiB vs. 2.24 GiB (100.0x less payload).
  Excludes index nodes, linked-list objects, lookup tables, and allocator overhead; measure process memory for real deployments.
  Orientation-key capacity is limited by precision, not a finite hash space. To preserve nearest-neighbor identity, total angular/query error ε should satisfy 2ε < δ_min, the minimum point separation.
  C) Uniform grid: O(n) build and O(n + g) space for point indices and g occupied buckets; expected near O(1) query under bounded density, worst-case O(n) point checks.
     Outward cell search is exact; cost depends on cells visited (cell width 0.25).

BEST FOR THIS MEASURED LOOKUP: B) Rotate points + array scan (0.119268 s total including index construction where applicable).
This timing is specific to the dataset size and machine; the invariant query and indexed data remain unchanged.


